{
  "arguments": [
    {
      "claim": "The protection afforded to St Ilven Hospital under Article 19 of the Fourth Geneva Convention and Article 13 of Additional Protocol I ceased because the hospital was used to commit acts harmful to the enemy that are not among the enumerated exemptions.",
      "reasoning": "Both the Convention and the Protocol state that protection does not cease unless the medical facility is used for acts harmful to the enemy, and that the loss of protection may occur only after such use. The exemptions listed (light personal weapons, pickets, small arms taken from the wounded, and combatants present for medical reasons) do not cover the use of the hospital as an artillery observation post, the mounting of a heavy machine gun on the roof, nor the storage of artillery ammunition in the basement. These constitute active participation in hostilities, i.e., acts harmful to the enemy, thereby lawfully terminating the hospital’s protected status.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "gc_iv",
          "article": "19",
          "quoted_text": "The protection to which civilian hospitals are entitled shall not cease unless they are used to commit, outside their humanitarian duties, acts harmful to the enemy. Protection may, however, cease only after due warning has been given, naming, in all appropriate cases, a reasonable time limit, and after such warning has remained unheeded."
        },
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "13",
          "quoted_text": "The protection to which civilian medical units are entitled shall not cease unless they are used to commit, outside their humanitarian function, acts harmful to the enemy. Protection may, however, cease only after a warning has been given setting, whenever appropriate, a reasonable time-limit, and after such warning has remained unheeded."
        },
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "13",
          "quoted_text": "that the personnel of the unit are equipped with light individual weapons for their own defence or for that of the wounded and sick in their charge; ... that small arms and ammunition taken from the wounded and sick, and not yet handed to the proper service, are found in the units;"
        },
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "12",
          "quoted_text": "Under no circumstances shall medical units be used in an attempt to shield military objectives from attack."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The warning issued by Calvaran forces on 9 August satisfied the requirement of a due warning with a reasonable time‑limit, and St Ilven Hospital failed to cease its hostile use, so the loss of protection remained in effect on 13 August.",
      "reasoning": "Article 19 of the Fourth Geneva Convention and Article 13 of Additional Protocol I require that a protected medical facility receive a warning that names a reasonable time‑limit before its protection can be revoked. The warning was transmitted by radio, leaflets, and an ICRC delegate, thereby meeting the “due warning” standard. The hospital continued to host an observation team, fire a heavy machine gun on 10 and 11 August, and store ammunition, demonstrating that the warning was unheeded. Consequently, at the time of the strike the hospital’s protection had lawfully ceased.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "gc_iv",
          "article": "19",
          "quoted_text": "The protection to which civilian hospitals are entitled shall not cease unless they are used to commit, outside their humanitarian duties, acts harmful to the enemy. Protection may, however, cease only after due warning has been given, naming, in all appropriate cases, a reasonable time limit, and after such warning has remained unheeded."
        },
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "13",
          "quoted_text": "The protection to which civilian medical units are entitled shall not cease unless they are used to commit, outside their humanitarian function, acts harmful to the enemy. Protection may, however, cease only after a warning has been given setting, whenever appropriate, a reasonable time-limit, and after such warning has remained unheeded."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The 13 August air strike complied with the principles of distinction and proportionality because it targeted only the parts of the hospital used for military purposes, employed precision‑guided munitions, and caused no civilian casualties.",
      "reasoning": "Even after protection has ceased, IHL obliges parties to direct attacks only at legitimate military objectives and to take all feasible precautions to avoid civilian harm. The strike was limited to the roof and top floor where the machine gun and observation post were located, used guided weapons to spare the lower floors, and no patients or civilian staff remained in the building. Accordingly, the attack was proportionate and distinguished between combatants and civilians, satisfying the remaining obligations of IHL.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "The continued protection of civilian hospitals under Article 18 of the Fourth Geneva Convention does not apply here because the hospital’s loss of protection under Article 19 was lawfully effected before the attack.",
      "reasoning": "Article 18 affirms that civilian hospitals may never be the object of attack while they retain protected status. However, Article 19 expressly provides a mechanism for that protection to cease when the hospital is used for hostile acts after a proper warning. Since the conditions of Article 19 were met, the general rule of Article 18 no longer governs the legality of the strike.",
      "citations": [
        {
